      "id": "dynamic-markings-from-pianissimo-to-fortissimo",
      "title": "Dynamic Markings: From Pianissimo to Fortissimo",
      "level": 1,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Track:** italian-terms | **Difficulty:** 1/10 | **Time:** ~8 min"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Dynamics indicate volume and its changes. Italian abbreviations are universal across classical, folk, and popular notation."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "static-dynamics",
      "title": "Static dynamics",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**pp** pianissimo — very soft. **p** piano — soft. **mp** mezzo piano — moderately soft. **mf** mezzo forte — moderately loud (common default). **f** forte — loud. **ff** fortissimo — very loud. **fff** — extreme loudness (rare)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "changing-dynamics",
      "title": "Changing dynamics",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "**Crescendo** (cresc. or <) — gradually louder. **Diminuendo/decrescendo** (dim., decresc., or >) — gradually softer. **Sforzando** (sf, sfz) — sudden accent. **Forte-piano** (fp) — loud attack then immediately soft."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "in-ensemble",
      "title": "In ensemble",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Dynamics interact with articulation and tempo. A reel at mf with crisp bowing differs from mp with sustain. Céilí band arrangements use strategic dynamic shifts to mark tune changes and solos."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
